# Set up Loupe

These steps set up Loupe at {{ instance }} for the current repository. Do the steps in order. Stop and ask the user when a step needs them. Never invent or write a token, a password or a URL that the user did not give you.

## 1. Check the operating system

The `loupe` CLI runs on macOS and Linux, on amd64 and arm64. It has no Windows build. If this machine runs Windows, stop and tell the user this.

## 2. Install the CLI

Run `loupe version`. If it prints a version, go to step 3.

If it fails, install the CLI with the install script of this Loupe. Run:

```bash
curl -fsSL {{ instance }}/install.sh | LOUPE_INSTALL_NO_TTY=1 sh
```

`LOUPE_INSTALL_NO_TTY=1` stops the script from asking questions. It installs the latest CLI release, into `~/.local/bin` unless `~/bin` is on the `PATH`. It checks the download against `checksums.txt` before it installs anything. It prints `Installed loupe <version> at <path>.` when it succeeds. If it fails, stop and show the output to the user.

After `Installed loupe`, the script prints the state of automatic updates, and the command that changes it. Show those lines to the user.

The MCP server starts as `loupe mcp`, so the directory of `loupe` must be on the `PATH` of the agent. If the script prints `is not on your PATH`, show the user the line it prints, and ask them to add it to their shell profile and restart the agent from a new terminal. Then stop. Run `loupe version` again. It must print a version.

## 3. Run loupe init

Run this step from the root of the repository.

`loupe init` does the rest of the setup. It signs in, chooses the project and writes `.loupe.yaml`. It declares the `loupe` MCP server to Claude Code or Codex, and installs the Loupe plugin in Claude Code or the Loupe skills for Codex. It writes the rule file `rules.yaml` and the starter profile `.loupe/lifecycle.md`, and it starts the bridge in the background. Each step skips itself when it is done already, so you can run the command again.

`loupe init` waits until the user approves the sign-in in a browser, so start it in the background, with its output in a log file. `nohup` keeps it alive when your shell call ends. `< /dev/null` tells it that no person answers questions. `--mcp` makes it declare the MCP server without asking.

{% if projectId -%}
The project id is `{{ id }}`. Run:

```bash
nohup loupe init --url {{ instance }} --project {{ id }} --mcp > /tmp/loupe-init.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &
```

If `.loupe.yaml` names another project, `loupe init` stops. Ask the user before you add `--force`.
{%- else -%}
Run:

```bash
nohup loupe init --url {{ instance }} --mcp > /tmp/loupe-init.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &
```

If `.loupe.yaml` exists, it names the project. If your login covers one or more projects, `loupe init` stops and lists them. Show the list to the user and ask whether to use one of them or to create a new project. To use one, run the command again with `--project <project id>`, with the chosen id in place of `<project id>`. To create one, ask the user for the project name, the domain and the origins that the site-review widget may run on. Then run the command again with `--name "<name>"`, and add `--domain <domain>` and one `--origin <origin>` for each origin, when the user gave them. If `loupe init` answers `unknown flag: --name`, run `loupe update` and run it again. If your login covers no project, `loupe init` creates one from the `origin` remote of the repository. With no remote, it asks for `--project`. Ask the user for the project id, or ask the user to create a project at {{ instance }} in a browser.
{%- endif %}

Read `/tmp/loupe-init.log` until it shows a page URL and a code. Show both to the user, and ask them to open the page, check the code and choose Allow. If the log shows no page URL, the sign-in exists already. Then read the log again until the command ends. It prints one line for each step, which says `done`, `skipped` or `failed`, and why. Show the lines to the user.

- If a step prints a command for you to run, such as `claude plugin install loupe@loupe`, run it, or show it to the user.
- If the output says another server already has the name `loupe`, show the output to the user and ask before you replace it.
- With no terminal, `loupe init` turns automatic updates off and skips the Playwright browser. Tell the user, and give them `loupe update` and `loupe init` for later.
- If the command ends with an error, show the output to the user and stop.

Tell the user to commit `.loupe/lifecycle.md`, because a worker sees only what the user pushes.

## 4. Check

Run `loupe status`. It asks Loupe for the current project and prints its name. It checks the sign-in and the project of this repository. It does not check the command that your agent starts, so step 5 is still necessary.

If the command is unknown, run `loupe update`, then run `loupe status` again. If it is still unknown, skip this step.

If `loupe status` fails, show its output to the user and stop.

## 5. Finish

Tell the user to restart the agent, because an agent reads its MCP servers and plugins when it starts. The new session does not see these steps, so give the user this prompt to paste into it:

```text
Call the Loupe tool project_current, then run `loupe status` in this repository. Tell me both project names. If they differ, tell me that the agent starts the Loupe MCP server for another project.
```
